  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 480,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 437,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67586,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 161,
        "headingId": 437,
        "headingText": "<p>The left atrium is the most common location, followed by the right atrium and the ventricular chambers, with the tumours being either intracavitary or infiltrating. When in the atria, cardiac leiomyosarcomas usually arise from the free wall&nbsp;and rarely have a stalk {25740020; 16399316; 11475835; 9209944}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-01-11T17:49:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 481,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 438,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67587,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 161,
        "headingId": 438,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Clinical presentation for left atrial tumours is often dyspnoea, haemoptysis, or atrial fibrillation, and for right-sided tumours is peripheral oedema and ascites. The median age is 48&nbsp;years (range: 6&nbsp;months to 86&nbsp;years), and there is no sex predominance {25740020}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:43:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 482,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 439,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67588,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 161,
        "headingId": 439,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Leiomyosarcoma accounts for &lt;&nbsp;20% of primary cardiac sarcomas in published series {9458144; 1434856}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:43:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 483,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 440,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67589,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 161,
        "headingId": 440,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Etiology has not been reported specifically for primary cardiac leiomyosarcomas.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:43:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 484,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 441,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67590,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 161,
        "headingId": 441,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pathogenesis has not been reported specifically for cardiac leiomyosarcomas. For information on general pathogenesis of leiomyosarcoma, see the <em>Soft tissue and bone tumours</em> volume of this series [[WHO Classification of Tumours Editorial Board. Soft tissue and bone tumours. Lyon (France): International Agency for Research on Cancer; 2020. (WHO classification of tumours series, 5th ed.; vol. 3). https://publications.iarc.fr/588.]].</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:43:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 491,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 448,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67591,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 161,
        "headingId": 448,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Leiomyosarcomas are homogeneous, firm, grey to white to tan masses, often multilobular. A whorled appearance may be evident. Variable necrosis can be present.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:43:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 485,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 442,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67592,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 161,
        "headingId": 442,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The majority of leiomyosarcomas are high-grade. They show spindle-shaped cells with plump, blunt-ended nuclei and moderate to abundant, pale to brightly eosinophilic fibrillary cytoplasm. The cells are set in long intersecting fascicles parallel and perpendicular to the plane of section; some tumours show areas with storiform or palisade patterns. Moderate nuclear pleomorphism is usually present. Mitotic figures, including atypical ones, are easy to find. Usually there is diffuse hypercellularity, although focal fibrosis, myxoid change, and hyalinized hypocellular areas can be present. There is often necrosis in larger tumours. By immunohistochemistry, SMA and desmin are typically positive {25740020; 23916929}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:43:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 486,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 443,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67593,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 161,
        "headingId": 443,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:43:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 487,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 444,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67594,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 161,
        "headingId": 444,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:43:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 490,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 447,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67595,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 161,
        "headingId": 447,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Fascicles of eosinophilic spindled cells with blunt-ended nuclei showing variable pleomorphism</li>\r\n<li>Immunolabelling for SMA, desmin, and/or caldesmon</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:43:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 488,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 445,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67596,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 161,
        "headingId": 445,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:43:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 489,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 446,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67597,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 161,
        "headingId": 446,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cardiac leiomyosarcomas are generally clinically aggressive neoplasms with frequent local recurrences and distant metastases. Adjuvant chemotherapy and radiation therapy is of unclear benefit in primary cardiac sarcomas. The completeness of excision in general affects survival. In a recent review of published cases, the 5-year overall survival rate and the local and metastatic recurrence-free survival rate were 25.4% and 14.7%, respectively {25740020}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-01T09:43:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]